        <!-- SECTION 2: Diameter, Length, and Water Flow -->
        <div id="diameter-and-flow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Diameter, Length, and Water Flow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two specifications determine how much water actually reaches the end of your hose, and buyers routinely get both wrong in the same direction: too narrow and too long.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diameter Determines Flow</h3>

          <p>
            Garden hoses come in three common inner diameters. Half inch is light and suits container watering and short runs. Five eighths inch is the standard for general home use and the right default, delivering good flow without excessive weight. Three quarter inch delivers the most water and is used for long runs, high-demand sprinklers, and commercial applications, at a considerable weight penalty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Length Costs You Pressure</h3>

          <p>
            Water loses pressure to friction as it travels through a hose, and that loss compounds with length. A 100-foot hose delivers noticeably less flow at the nozzle than a 50-foot hose of the same diameter connected to the same spigot. Buying more length than you need is the most common way people accidentally reduce their own water pressure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy the Shortest Hose That Reaches</h3>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to measure the farthest point you actually need to water and buy that length rather than the next size up for insurance. Two 50-foot hoses joined when needed is a better arrangement than one permanent 100-foot hose, because most of the time you run only the first fifty feet at full pressure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Go Up a Diameter</h3>

          <p>
            If you run oscillating or impact sprinklers, fill large containers, or need a run longer than 75 feet, stepping from 5/8 to 3/4 inch recovers a meaningful amount of flow. For hand watering with a nozzle, the difference is far less noticeable and the extra weight is not worth carrying.
          </p>

