        <!-- SECTION 4: Fittings and Couplings: Where Hoses Actually Fail -->
        <div id="fittings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fittings and Couplings: Where Hoses Actually Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ask anyone why they threw out their last hose and the answer is usually a leak at the spigot or a coupling that would not tighten. The tube outlasts the fittings on most cheap hoses.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Solid brass is the standard worth insisting on.</strong> Thin stamped brass and plastic couplings deform when wrenched, and once a coupling is out of round it will never seal properly again. Solid machined brass tolerates being tightened repeatedly and lasts as long as the hose does. Check the wall thickness by feel, since heavier is genuinely better here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anodized aluminum is a legitimate alternative.</strong> Lighter than brass, corrosion resistant, and less prone to galling when threaded onto an aluminum spigot. Flexzilla uses it well. The one caution is galvanic corrosion when aluminum is threaded directly onto certain dissimilar metals for long periods, which a washer and occasional disconnection prevents.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Look for a grip collar or octagonal fitting.</strong> A smooth cylindrical coupling is genuinely hard to tighten by hand, especially when wet. A ribbed grip collar or a hex shape lets you get it snug without a wrench, which reduces the temptation to over-tighten and crush the fitting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Crush-resistant designs matter at the spigot.</strong> The coupling nearest the faucet takes the most abuse from being stepped on, driven over, and wrenched. Some hoses use a reinforced or oversized fitting there specifically. If yours does not, a short leader hose absorbs that wear instead of the main hose.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Replace the Washer Before Replacing the Hose</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A leak at the spigot connection is almost always a failed rubber washer inside the coupling, not a failed hose. Washers cost pennies, take ten seconds to swap with a small screwdriver, and are the single most common reason people throw away a perfectly good hose. Keep a handful in the garage and check them at the start of every season.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Burst Pressure, Kinking, and Drinking Water Safety -->
        <div id="burst-and-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Burst Pressure, Kinking, and Drinking Water Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three specifications on a hose package deserve more attention than they get, and one of them is a genuine health consideration rather than a performance one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Burst pressure.</strong> This is the pressure at which the hose fails, and household water pressure typically sits between 40 and 80 PSI. A burst rating of 350 PSI or higher provides good margin for pressure spikes, for the hose being left pressurized with a closed nozzle in the sun, and for use with attachments. Cheap hoses often rate well below that, which is why they split at the coupling on a hot afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Kink resistance is about construction, not marketing.</strong> Every hose claims to be kink resistant. What actually determines it is the material stiffness and the reinforcement layer. Hybrid polymer and woven composite hoses genuinely resist kinking; vinyl hoses generally do not regardless of what the packaging says. The practical test is whether flow returns immediately when you release a forced bend, or whether the crease holds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drinking water safety is a real distinction.</strong> Hoses not certified for potable water can contain plasticizers, and lead in brass fittings, that leach into water standing in the hose, particularly when it heats in the sun. If anyone fills a pet bowl, a kiddie pool, a water bottle, or a watering can for edible plants from your hose, buy a hose specifically labeled drinking water safe with lead-free fittings.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Always flush a hose before drinking from it.</strong> Even with a certified hose, water that has been sitting in a hot hose is warm and stale. Run it for fifteen or twenty seconds to clear the standing water before filling anything anyone will drink from. This is good practice regardless of the hose you own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Matching a Hose to How You Use It -->
        <div id="matching-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching a Hose to How You Use It
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">General Yard Watering</h3>

          <p>
            A 5/8-inch hybrid polymer hose in the length that actually reaches your farthest target. This covers hand watering, sprinklers, washing a car, and most other household tasks with the best balance of flow, weight, and kink resistance. Flexzilla is the default answer here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Container Gardens and Patios</h3>

          <p>
            A smaller-diameter polyurethane hose. Flow matters less when you are filling pots by hand, and the reduced weight and tighter coiling make it far more pleasant to use in a confined space. Drinking water safety is also more relevant here since you may be watering edibles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Workshops, Garages, and Farm Use</h3>

          <p>
            Rubber, without hesitation. Abrasion from concrete, being run over, connection to pressure washers, and hot water use are all things rubber handles and polymer hoses do not. Accept the weight as the price of a hose that survives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Long Runs to the Back of a Property</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to 3/4-inch diameter to recover the flow lost to friction, and consider running a permanent line with a second spigot if the distance is significant. A 100-foot hose at 5/8 inch delivers disappointing flow for sprinklers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anyone With Grip or Strength Limitations</h3>

          <p>
            Weight and coil memory matter more than anything else. A lightweight woven composite or small-diameter polyurethane hose, paired with a hose reel that does the coiling for you, transforms a difficult task into a manageable one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Care, Storage, and Winter Protection -->
        <div id="care-and-winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Care, Storage, and Winter Protection
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hoses rarely die of old age. They die of sun, freezing, and being stored under tension, all of which are avoidable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not leave a hose pressurized.</strong> Shutting the nozzle and leaving the spigot on traps water under full pressure, and a hose lying in the sun with trapped water can see pressure rise well above supply pressure as the water heats. This is the most common cause of a hose bursting at a coupling. Turn the spigot off and relieve pressure at the nozzle when you finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it out of direct sun.</strong> Ultraviolet exposure is what degrades vinyl and, more slowly, most other materials. A hose reel in shade, a wall hook under an eave, or a hose pot roughly doubles the service life of a vinyl hose compared to leaving it coiled on a sunny driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coil without twisting.</strong> Repeatedly coiling a hose the same direction builds twist into it, which is what makes a hose fight you when you lay it out. Coiling in a figure-eight pattern, or using a reel that lets the hose rotate, prevents accumulated twist entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drain before freezing weather.</strong> Water expanding as it freezes inside a hose splits the inner tube and destroys the hose, and it can damage the spigot too. Disconnect from the spigot, stretch the hose out downhill to drain fully, then coil it loosely and store it somewhere it will not freeze solid. Woven-jacket hoses in particular need thorough draining.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not hang a full hose from a single small hook.</strong> Concentrating the weight of a water-filled hose on one narrow point creates a permanent kink at that spot. Use a wide-radius hanger or a reel, both of which support the hose across a curve rather than a point.
          </p>
        </div>

