              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What diameter garden hose should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Five eighths inch is the right default for general home use, balancing flow and weight. Half inch is lighter and suits container watering and short runs but delivers less water. Three quarter inch is worth stepping up to for runs longer than about 75 feet, for oscillating sprinklers, or for filling large containers quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my garden hose keep kinking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kinking is mostly a function of material. Vinyl hoses kink readily and hold the crease, while hybrid polymer and woven composite hoses resist kinking and recover flow when tension releases. Accumulated twist from always coiling the same direction makes it worse, so coil in a figure-eight pattern or use a reel that lets the hose rotate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is garden hose water safe to drink?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only from a hose specifically labeled drinking water safe with lead-free fittings. Standard vinyl hoses can leach plasticizers into water standing in a hot hose, and some brass fittings contain lead. Polyurethane hoses like the Eley and Water Right models are certified for potable water. Even with a certified hose, flush it for fifteen to twenty seconds before filling anything.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do garden hoses last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A basic vinyl hose left in the sun typically lasts around three years before stiffening and cracking. A quality rubber, hybrid polymer, or polyurethane hose stored out of direct sun and drained before winter commonly lasts ten years or more. Sun exposure and freezing with water inside are the two things that kill hoses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my hose leaking at the spigot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always a failed rubber washer inside the hose coupling. Pull the old washer out with a small screwdriver, push a new one in, and hand-tighten the connection. Washers cost pennies and are the most common reason a working hose gets thrown away. If the leak persists, check whether the coupling has been crushed out of round.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Garden Hose Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hoses fail for a small number of predictable reasons, and almost all of them are habits rather than product defects.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Hose Pressurized in the Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shutting the nozzle while leaving the spigot on traps water under pressure, and as that water heats in the sun the pressure climbs well above supply pressure. This is the single most common cause of a hose bursting at a coupling. Turn the spigot off and open the nozzle briefly to relieve pressure when you finish watering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying More Length Than You Need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water loses pressure to friction over distance, so a 100-foot hose delivers noticeably weaker flow at the nozzle than a 50-foot one on the same spigot. Measure to the farthest point you actually water and buy that length. Two shorter hoses joined when needed beat one long hose that costs you pressure every day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a Hose With Water Inside Over Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water expands as it freezes and splits the inner tube, destroying the hose and sometimes damaging the spigot too. Disconnect, stretch the hose out to drain fully, then coil loosely and store somewhere protected. This one habit is the difference between a hose lasting one winter and ten.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging a Hose by the Tube and Ignoring the Fittings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most cheap hoses fail at the coupling, not in the middle. Thin stamped brass and plastic fittings deform when wrenched and never seal again once out of round. Solid machined brass or anodized aluminum fittings cost a little more up front and outlast several cheap hoses.
              </p>
